SRC=['ityākarṇya vaco devyāḥ sa dūto’marṣapūritaḥ | / samācaṣṭa samāgamya daityarājāya vistarāt || 2||',
'tasya dūtasya tadvākyamākarṇyāsurarāṭ tataḥ | / sakrodhaḥ prāha daityānāmadhipaṁ dhūmralocanam || 3||',
'he dhūmralocanāśu tvaṁ svasainyaparivāritaḥ | / tāmānaya balādduṣṭāṁ keśākarṣaṇavihvalām || 4||',
'tatparitrāṇadaḥ kaścidyadi vottiṣṭhate paraḥ | / sa hantavyo’maro vāpi yakṣo gandharva eva vā || 5||',
'tenājñaptastataḥ śīghraṁ sa daityo dhūmralocanaḥ | / vṛtaḥ ṣaṣṭyā sahasrāṇāmasurāṇāṁ drutaṁ yayau || 7||',
'sa dṛṣṭvā tāṁ tato devīṁ tuhinācalasaṁsthitām | / jagādoccaiḥ prayāhīti mūlaṁ śumbhaniśumbhayoḥ || 8||',
'na cetprītyādya bhavatī madbhartāramupaiṣyati | / tato balānnayāmyeṣa keśākarṣaṇavihvalām || 9||',
'daityeśvareṇa prahito balavānbalasaṁvṛtaḥ | / balānnayasi māmevaṁ tataḥ kiṁ te karomyaham || 11||',
'ityuktaḥ so’bhyadhāvattāmasuro dhūmralocanaḥ | / huṅkāreṇaiva taṁ bhasma sā cakārāmbikā tadā || 13||',
'atha kruddhaṁ mahāsainyamasurāṇāṁ tathāmbikā | / vavarṣa sāyakaistīkṣṇaistathā śaktiparaśvadhaiḥ || 14||',
'tato dhutasaṭaḥ kopātkṛtvā nādaṁ subhairavam | / papātāsurasenāyāṁ siṁho devyāḥ svavāhanaḥ || 15||',
'kāṁścitkaraprahāreṇa daityānāsyena cāparān | / ākrāntyā cādhareṇānyān jaghāna sa mahāsurān || 16||',
'keṣāñcitpāṭayāmāsa nakhaiḥ koṣṭhāni kesarī | / tathā talaprahāreṇa śirāṁsi kṛtavānpṛthak || 17||',
'vicchinnabāhuśirasaḥ kṛtāstena tathāpare | / papau ca rudhiraṁ koṣṭhādanyeṣāṁ dhutakesaraḥ || 18||',
'kṣaṇena tadbalaṁ sarvaṁ kṣayaṁ nītaṁ mahātmanā | / tena kesariṇā devyā vāhanenātikopinā || 19||',
'śrutvā tamasuraṁ devyā nihataṁ dhūmralocanam | / balaṁ ca kṣayitaṁ kṛtsnaṁ devīkesariṇā tataḥ || 20|| / cukopa daityādhipatiḥ śumbhaḥ prasphuritādharaḥ | / ājñāpayāmāsa ca tau caṇḍamuṇḍau mahāsurau || 21||',
'he caṇḍa he muṇḍa balairbahubhiḥ parivāritau | / tatra gacchata gatvā ca sā samānīyatāṁ laghu || 22|| / keśeṣvākṛṣya baddhvā vā yadi vaḥ saṁśayo yudhi | / tadāśeṣāyudhaiḥ sarvairasurairvinihanyatām || 23||',
'tasyāṁ hatāyāṁ duṣṭāyāṁ siṁhe ca vinipātite | / śīghramāgamyatāṁ baddhvā gṛhītvā tāmathāmbikām || 24||']
def num(s): return re.findall(r'\|\|\s*(\d+)\s*\|\|',s)
FV=[dict(n=('–'.join([num(s)[0],num(s)[-1]]) if len(num(s))>1 else num(s)[0]),lines=L(s)) for s in SRC]
def S(*ns): return ' / '.join(s for s in SRC if any(x in num(s) for x in ns))
V=[
VP(C,'2–5',S('2','3','4','5'),'“DRAG HER BY THE HAIR”',
 sc('Shumbha on his throne shouting orders at a smoky-eyed general, a messenger kneeling beside them',BATTLE+'<rect x="20" y="40" width="70" height="80" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+ASURA(55,110,.9,'#3a5a6a')+ASURA(150,150,.9,'#555')+'<circle cx="146" cy="96" r="4" fill="#bbb"/><circle cx="156" cy="96" r="4" fill="#bbb"/>'+SPEECH(150,30,'BRING HER!',90,11)),
 'Hearing the Devī’s words, the messenger, full of indignation, went back and told the king of the daityas everything in detail. Hearing his report, the asura king, in anger, said to Dhūmralocana, chief of the daityas: “Dhūmralocana, go quickly with your army and bring that wicked woman here by force, distraught, dragged by her hair. If anyone rises to protect her, be he god, yakṣa or gandharva, he must be killed.”',
 [('amarṣa-pūritaḥ','<b>full of indignation</b>'),('dhūmra-locana','<b>“smoke-eyed”</b>'),('balāt','<b>by force</b>'),('keśa-ākarṣaṇa','<b>dragging by the hair</b>'),('paritrāṇa-da','<b>a protector</b>')],
 '<b>The insult.</b> Śumbha does not hear a challenge; he hears a woman refusing him. His answer is to send force: drag her by the hair. Note the name of his general: <b>Dhūmralocana</b>, “smoke-eyed”, whose sight is clouded.',
 'Pride hears a “no” and <b>reaches for force</b>.'),
VP(C,'7–9',S('7','8','9'),'SIXTY THOUSAND ASURAS',
 sc('A smoky-eyed general with a huge army climbing toward the Goddess seated calmly on her lion on a snowy peak',HIMA+DEVI(170,0,.5,False)+LION(170,90,.4)+ASURA(70,150,1.0,'#555')+''.join(ASURA(20+k*14,150-(k%2)*6,.35,'#3a2a6a') for k in range(8))+T(60,30,'60,000',14)),
 'So commanded, the daitya Dhūmralocana went swiftly, surrounded by sixty thousand asuras. Seeing the Devī standing on the snowy mountain, he shouted: “Come to Śumbha and Niśumbha! If you will not come to my master gladly today, I will take you by force, distraught, dragged by your hair.”',
 [('ṣaṣṭyā sahasrāṇām','<b>with sixty thousand</b>'),('tuhina-acala','<b>the snowy mountain</b>'),('prayāhi','<b>go! come along!</b>'),('prītyā','<b>willingly</b>'),('bhartāram','<b>(my) master</b>')],
 '<b>Willingly or by force.</b> The same false choice every bully offers. Dhūmralocana repeats his master’s threat word for word: he has no thought of his own.',
 '“Come willingly, <b>or be dragged</b>.”'),
VP(C,'11',S('11'),'“THEN WHAT CAN I DO?”',
 sc('The Goddess on the mountain with her hands open, a gentle ironic smile on her face, the general shouting below',HIMA+DEVI(130,6,.7,False)+ASURA(50,150,.9,'#555')+SPEECH(150,24,'what can I do?',100,10)),
 'The Devī said: “Sent by the lord of the daityas, mighty yourself and surrounded by an army: if you take me by force like this, then what can I do to you?”',
 [('prahitaḥ','<b>sent</b>'),('balavān bala-saṃvṛtaḥ','<b>mighty, and surrounded by might</b>'),('kiṃ te karomi aham','<b>what can I do to you?</b>')],
 '<b>Deadpan.</b> She plays helpless: “You are so strong, with such an army, what can I possibly do?” It is irony, like her “little understanding” in chapter 5. The next verse shows exactly what she can do.',
 'Her humility <b>is a trap of irony</b>.'),
VP(C,'13',S('13'),'“HUṂ”',
 sc('The Goddess uttering a single syllable HUM, and the charging general turning into a puff of ash',HIMA+DEVI(150,6,.7,False)+'<path d="M120 50 q-30 0 -50 40" fill="none" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>'+T(100,40,'HUṂ!',20,'#c0392b')+''.join(f'<circle cx="{40+k*7}" cy="{120-(k%3)*10}" r="{4+k%3}" fill="#999" opacity=".7" stroke="none"/>' for k in range(8))),
 'Spoken to thus, the asura Dhūmralocana rushed at her; and Ambikā, with a mere “huṃ”, reduced him to ashes.',
 [('abhyadhāvat','<b>rushed at</b>'),('huṃ-kāreṇa eva','<b>with just the sound “huṃ”</b>'),('bhasma cakāra','<b>made into ashes</b>')],
 '<b>One syllable.</b> No weapon, no lion: just “huṃ”. In mantra tradition <i>huṃ</i> is a powerful seed syllable of protection and destruction. The general whose eyes were clouded with smoke is himself turned to ash.',
 'One syllable from her <b>turns pride to ash</b>.'),
VP(C,'14–15',S('14','15'),'THE LION LEAPS',
 sc('The Goddess raining arrows and axes on the asura army while her lion leaps into it with a terrible roar',BATTLE+DEVI(170,4,.5)+ARROWS(80,60,5)+LION(90,110,1.0)+''.join(ASURA(20+k*30,150,.5,'#3a2a6a') for k in range(3))+T(90,74,'ROAR!',12,'#c0392b')),
 'Then Ambikā rained sharp arrows, spears and axes on the great, enraged army of the asuras. Then the Devī’s own mount, the lion, shaking its mane in anger and giving a most terrible roar, fell upon the asura army.',
 [('sāyaka','<b>arrows</b>'),('paraśvadha','<b>axes</b>'),('dhuta-saṭaḥ','<b>shaking its mane</b>'),('su-bhairavam','<b>most terrible</b>'),('sva-vāhana','<b>her own mount</b>')],
 '<b>The lion again.</b> As in chapter 3, her vāhana fights as a hero in his own right. Sixty thousand asuras, and she leaves most of them to the lion.',
 'Her lion alone <b>is more than an army</b>.'),
VP(C,'16–19',S('16','17','18','19'),'SIXTY THOUSAND IN A MOMENT',
 sc('The lion with paws, jaws and claws scattering the asura army, which vanishes in a flash',BATTLE+LION(110,110,1.3)+''.join(f'<path d="M{110+60*math.cos(k*0.8):.0f} {90+50*math.sin(k*0.8):.0f} l{10*math.cos(k*0.8):.0f} {10*math.sin(k*0.8):.0f}" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(8))+T(110,24,'IN A MOMENT',13)),
 'Some daityas it killed with blows of its paw, others with its jaws, others by trampling them under its hind feet. The lion tore open the bellies of some with its claws and with a slap struck off the heads of others. Others it left without arms and heads; and shaking its mane, it drank the blood from the bellies of others. In a moment the whole army was destroyed by that great-souled, furious lion, the Devī’s mount.',
 [('kara-prahāra','<b>blow of the paw</b>'),('āsya','<b>mouth, jaws</b>'),('nakha','<b>claws</b>'),('kṣaṇena','<b>in a moment</b>'),('mahātman','<b>great-souled</b>')],
 '<b>“Great-souled” lion.</b> The text calls the lion <i>mahātman</i>, a word used for saints. The fierce details are the Purāṇa’s way of saying: what serves her is itself holy, and nothing stands against it.',
 'Even her lion is called <b>great-souled</b>.'),
VP(C,'20–24',S('20','22','24'),'ŚUMBHA SENDS CAṆḌA AND MUṆḌA',
 sc('Shumbha biting his trembling lip in fury, pointing two huge asuras, Chanda and Munda, toward the mountain',BATTLE+'<rect x="10" y="40" width="70" height="80" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111"/>'+ASURA(45,110,.9,'#3a5a6a')+ASURA(130,150,1.0,'#7a2a2a')+ASURA(180,150,1.0,'#2a2a7a')+T(130,40,'CAṆḌA',11)+T(180,40,'MUṆḌA',11)),
 'Hearing that the asura Dhūmralocana was slain by the Devī and his whole army destroyed by her lion, Śumbha, lord of the daityas, grew angry, his lip trembling, and commanded the two great asuras Caṇḍa and Muṇḍa: “Caṇḍa! Muṇḍa! Go there with large forces and bring her quickly, dragged by the hair or bound. If you are in doubt in the fight, then let all the asuras strike her with every weapon. When that wicked woman is wounded and the lion struck down, seize Ambikā, bind her and come back quickly.”',
 [('prasphurita-adharaḥ','<b>his lip trembling</b>'),('caṇḍa · muṇḍa','<b>two great asura generals</b>'),('laghu','<b>quickly</b>'),('baddhvā','<b>having bound her</b>'),('saṃśaya','<b>doubt</b>')],
 '<b>Learning nothing.</b> One general has become ash with a single sound; Śumbha’s answer is to send two bigger ones with bigger armies. Notice the new hedge: “if you are in doubt in the fight”. Fear is starting to creep in.',
 'Pride doubles its bet <b>instead of learning</b>.'),
]
